<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Spanish Jamaica</h1><p class="st-meta">1509 CE – 1655 CE · capital Villa de la Vega</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Spain's lightly settled colony of Santiago on Jamaica, held for 146 years mainly for cattle and provisions until the English conquest of 1655.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> reached <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in 1494 and was stranded there in 1503-04. In 1509 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-esquivel">Juan de Esquivel</a>, sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-columbus">Diego Colón</a>, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sevilla-la-nueva">Sevilla la Nueva</a> on the north coast. Around 1534 the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-town">Villa de la Vega</a>, later Spanish Town, on the southern plain.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The island was the hereditary domain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> family. With no gold, it became a supply base raising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, pigs and horses and producing hides, lard and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> for passing fleets and the conquest of the mainland. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> population was nearly wiped out within decades, and enslaved Africans were brought in. The Spanish population remained small.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1655 an English expedition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sir-william-penn">Penn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-venables">Venables</a> took the island. Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cristobal-arnaldo-ysasi">Cristóbal Arnaldo Ysasi</a> resisted until 1660, and freed Africans formed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jamaican-maroons">Jamaican Maroons</a>. The island became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-jamaica">Colony of Jamaica</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/spanish-jamaica.jpg" alt="Spanish Jamaica" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Connormah · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jamaica_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1509 CE – 1655 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Villa de la Vega</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Juan de Esquivel, Cristóbal Arnaldo Ysasi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/jamaica">Jamaica</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Santiago, Colony of Santiago</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1582&amp;focus=spanish-jamaica" aria-label="Spanish Jamaica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2581 1503 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Spanish Jamaica lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2901 1759L4910 989M2901 1759L4997 651M2901 1759L2917 1766"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M4997 651h0M2917 1766h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2901 1759h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2901 1759h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1582&amp;focus=spanish-jamaica">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
